<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teatro di San Carlo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1737">1737</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Teatro di San Carlo is the opera house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, opened on 4 November 1737 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles of Bourbon</a> next to the royal palace. It is the oldest opera house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> still in continuous use and was a leading centre of Italian opera, with works by Rossini and Donizetti premiered there. After a fire in 1816 it was rebuilt within a year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teatro-di-san-carlo.jpg" alt="Teatro di San Carlo" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teatro_de_San_Carlos,_N%C3%A1poles,_Italia,_2023-03-25,_DD_184-186_HDR.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
